          Most people buy a hygrometer after something goes wrong: a musty basement, a foggy window, a mold spot behind the couch. The right one costs less than a takeout dinner, and the difference between a good one and a bad one is not the display. It is whether the number is within a few percent of the truth and whether it remembers what happened while you were asleep.
        </p>

