        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A hygrometer has one job, and it is easy to judge: does the number match reality closely enough to act on? We focused on that first. Every candidate had to be a current, widely sold model with a published humidity accuracy rating, and we ran each through a saturated salt test, which holds the air in a sealed bag at a known 75 percent relative humidity, to see whether it lived up to that rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            We then set the survivors side by side in three places with very different conditions: a finished bedroom, an unfinished basement running a dehumidifier, and a garage that swings with the weather. Watching them together for several weeks showed which ones agreed with each other, which responded quickly to a change like a hot shower or a door opening, and which drifted or lagged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Accuracy alone does not decide which hygrometer is most useful, though. A sensor that logs every few minutes and lets you scroll back through a wet week answers questions a display never can. A Wi-Fi sensor that texts you when a crawl space passes 60 percent prevents problems a more accurate standalone unit would only report after the fact. So we weighed history, alerts and ease of reading nearly as heavily as the sensor spec.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out analog dial hygrometers, which are routinely off by 5 to 10 percent and difficult to calibrate, and very cheap unbranded digital units whose accuracy was no better than a guess. We also left out professional instruments costing several times more than anything here, since a homeowner does not need laboratory precision to decide whether a basement needs a dehumidifier.
          </p>
