        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Hygrometer Accuracy Really Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hygrometer boxes quote accuracy as plus or minus a percentage of relative humidity, usually 2 or 3 percent for decent consumer units. That means a hygrometer showing 50 percent with a 3 percent tolerance could be sitting in a room anywhere from 47 to 53 percent. For deciding whether a basement is too damp, that range is fine. For a humidor or an instrument case, where the target is a narrow band, it is the difference between right and wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the fine print, because the headline number rarely applies everywhere. The ThermoPro TP50 is rated at plus or minus 2 percent between 30 and 80 percent RH and 3 percent outside that band. SensorPush quotes 3 percent typical and 4.5 percent maximum in the middle range. Almost every sensor gets less accurate near the extremes, at very low or very high humidity, and in cold temperatures.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">HOW CLOSE IS CLOSE ENOUGH</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">±2%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Good consumer spec, 30 to 80% RH</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">±3%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical smart-sensor spec</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">75%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Salt test reference humidity</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">±5-10%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common error of analog dials</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sensors also drift. Capacitive humidity sensors, the kind in every digital hygrometer here, slowly shift over months and years, especially after long exposure to very damp air. That is not a defect; it is how the technology ages. It is the reason we recommend a salt test when a new hygrometer arrives and again once a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, give any hygrometer time. Carry one from a warm kitchen to a cool basement and it may take 20 to 30 minutes to settle, because both the sensor and the plastic housing have to reach the new temperature. A reading taken the moment you set it down is not a reading you should act on.
          </p>
        </div>

