          <p>
            Basements deserve particular attention. They sit against cool soil, so warm, humid summer air that enters them cools and its relative humidity rises, sometimes to the point of condensation on walls and ductwork. A basement that reads 58 percent in the middle of the room can be well above 70 percent against the foundation wall. That is why we suggest aiming for 45 to 50 percent in the open air of the basement, which leaves a margin at the cold surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a dehumidifier is doing the work, the hygrometer tells you whether its setting matches reality. Built-in humidistats are often off by 5 percent or more because they sit in the machine's own airflow. Set the dehumidifier, check the hygrometer a few feet away, and adjust; our guide on <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> explains how to find the setting that actually holds the room at target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="salt-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Calibrate a Hygrometer With the Salt Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            The salt test is the cheapest calibration method there is, and it is surprisingly precise. A saturated solution of ordinary table salt holds the air above it at about 75 percent relative humidity across normal room temperatures. Seal a hygrometer in with it long enough and the reading should settle at 75. Whatever it shows instead is your error.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put a few tablespoons of plain salt in a bottle cap or small dish and add just enough water to make a damp paste, like wet sand, with undissolved crystals still visible. Place the dish and the hygrometer together in a large zip-top bag or an airtight food container, making sure the salt cannot spill onto the sensor. Seal it and leave it somewhere with a steady temperature, away from sun and heating vents.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Wait at least 8 hours, ideally overnight, before reading it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The air in the bag needs time to reach equilibrium and the sensor needs time to settle. Reading it after an hour usually shows a number still climbing, which leads people to apply the wrong correction.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the hygrometer reads 75, it is accurate at that point. If it reads 72, it is reading 3 percent low, and you should add 3 to every reading. Some models, including the AcuRite 01083 and the Inkbird IBS-TH2, let you enter that offset so the display corrects itself. For others, a small piece of tape on the back with the correction written on it works.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep in mind that a salt test checks one point. A sensor that is 3 percent low at 75 percent is probably close to 3 percent low at 50, but not always exactly. If you need precision across a range, two-way humidity calibration packs at different levels are sold for exactly this purpose. For a home, a yearly salt test is plenty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smart-vs-standalone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Smart vs Standalone Hygrometers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standalone hygrometer is a sensor and a screen. You look at it, it tells you the humidity. Most also record the high and low since you last reset them. They are cheap, the batteries last a long time, and there is nothing to pair or update. Their weakness is that they only help when someone looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bluetooth models add a log. The sensor stores readings, and when your phone comes within range the app downloads them and draws a graph. That turns a snapshot into a story: you see that the basement hits 65 percent every afternoon when the sun heats the south wall, or that the bedroom climbs to 60 overnight with the door shut. Bluetooth alerts work only while your phone is nearby, which is fine for a house you live in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wi-Fi models, or Bluetooth sensors paired with a Wi-Fi gateway, send data to the cloud continuously and can alert you anywhere. That is the feature that prevents damage rather than documenting it. A dehumidifier that trips its breaker, a full bucket that stops the unit, a sump pit that starts to overflow: each shows up as a humidity climb, and a Wi-Fi sensor tells you within minutes instead of weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: standalone units for rooms you are in every day, Bluetooth loggers for basements and bedrooms you want to understand, and Wi-Fi sensors for crawl spaces, second homes and anywhere a problem could run for days before anyone notices. Most households are well served by one of each type in the right places.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="logging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Why Logging Beats a Single Reading
          </h2>

          <p>
            Humidity in a house moves constantly. It rises with cooking, showers, laundry, breathing and rain; it falls when the air conditioner or dehumidifier runs and when dry winter air leaks in. Glancing at a screen once a day catches a random point on that curve, and the random point is usually the least useful one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold does not care about the average you see at breakfast. It cares about how many hours a surface spends damp. A basement that reads 50 percent every time you walk past it but sits at 68 percent every night while the dehumidifier cycles off is a basement with a mold problem waiting to happen. Only a log shows that pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Logs also make troubleshooting fast. After you add a dehumidifier, the graph shows how long it takes to pull the room down and whether it holds. After you seal a basement wall or regrade a downspout, comparing the week after the next heavy rain with the week before tells you whether it worked. When a contractor asks how bad the humidity gets, you can show them rather than guess.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much history you need depends on the question. Twenty days is enough to see daily patterns and the effect of a change. A year shows seasons, which is how you learn that the basement only needs the dehumidifier from May to October. Exporting to a spreadsheet, which the Govee, SensorPush and Inkbird apps support, lets you keep records indefinitely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Where to Put a Hygrometer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put the hygrometer where people live and breathe, at roughly shoulder or chest height, in the middle of the space you care about. That gives the most representative reading. Avoid direct sun, the top of a refrigerator or TV, and anywhere near a heat register, a humidifier's mist, a dehumidifier's exhaust or a bathroom door, all of which create local conditions that do not reflect the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep it off exterior walls in winter. The inside surface of an outside wall is cooler than the room, so a hygrometer mounted there reads higher humidity than the air a few feet away. That can be useful if you are hunting condensation, but it will make the room seem damper than it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a basement, place the main hygrometer in the open area, away from the dehumidifier by at least ten feet, so you are measuring the room and not the machine. If you want to know how damp the cold surfaces get, a second sensor on a shelf near the foundation wall or in a closed storage room is informative. The gap between the two readings tells you how much margin your target really has.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a crawl space, set the sensor on a block or hang it from a joist so it is not lying on the vapor barrier, and make sure you can reach it to change batteries. Wi-Fi or a gateway is almost essential here, because nobody checks a crawl space hygrometer in person often enough.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="basements" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Using a Hygrometer in Basements and Crawl Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Basements are where a hygrometer earns its money, because most basement moisture problems start as humidity rather than standing water. A persistent musty smell, efflorescence on block walls, rusting tool steel and cardboard that goes soft are all signs of air that stays too damp for too long. A hygrometer converts those signs into a number you can target.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by measuring for a week without changing anything. If the basement regularly exceeds 60 percent, it needs active dehumidification; if it sits between 50 and 60, ventilation and sealing may be enough. Our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> lays out the order to tackle the sources, from gutters and grading to sealing and air movement.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a dehumidifier is warranted, the hygrometer data tells you how big a unit you need. A basement that climbs 10 percent in a few hours after rain needs more capacity than one that creeps up slowly. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best dehumidifiers for basements</a> compares units by real pint capacity, and a logged hygrometer is the best way to confirm the one you choose keeps up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces follow the same rules with less margin, because they are usually colder, darker and closer to the soil. Keep them under 60 percent and ideally around 50 to 55 percent, and watch for spikes after rain or when summer air enters through vents. Encapsulated crawl spaces with a dehumidifier should hold a very steady line on the graph; a sawtooth pattern means something is leaking in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity and soil gas often share the same entry points, so if you are already monitoring a basement, it is worth adding a <a href="/best-radon-detector/">radon detector</a> too. Several of the better radon monitors log humidity as well, and seeing both rise together is a good clue about where the house is breathing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seasons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Humidity Through the Seasons
          </h2>

          <p>
            Summer is the humid season in most of the country. Warm air holds a lot of moisture, and when it reaches a cool basement it cools and its relative humidity climbs. This is when basement dehumidifiers work hardest and when a hygrometer with alerts is most valuable. Opening basement windows on a muggy day usually makes things worse, not better, and a hygrometer will prove it within an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter flips the problem. Cold outdoor air holds very little moisture, so once it leaks in and warms up, indoor relative humidity drops, often below 30 percent in heated homes. The exception is basements that stay damp from ground moisture, and houses where the problem is condensation on cold windows. In cold climates, keeping living areas at 30 to 40 percent in winter avoids window condensation without drying everyone out.
          </p>

          <p>
            That seasonal shift is why many people wonder whether to keep the dehumidifier running in winter. The answer depends on what your hygrometer says, not the calendar; our guide on whether <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/">you need a dehumidifier in winter</a> walks through the readings that justify it. Many basements that need 24-hour dehumidification in July can switch it off from December to March.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spring and fall are the transition seasons, and they are where a logged hygrometer pays for itself. The graph shows exactly when the basement starts climbing in spring, so you can start the dehumidifier before the musty smell arrives, and when it settles in fall, so you are not paying to run a machine that has nothing to remove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the dehumidifier's own humidistat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The sensor inside a dehumidifier sits in the machine's own airflow and is often 5 percent or more off. Setting it to 50 and assuming the room is at 50 is how basements stay musty. Place a separate hygrometer several feet away and adjust the dehumidifier until the hygrometer, not the machine, shows your target.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the salt test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new hygrometer can arrive reading 3 to 5 percent off, and all of them drift with age. An overnight salt test in a sealed bag costs almost nothing and tells you exactly how far off yours is. Do it when the hygrometer arrives and once a year, and write the correction on the back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading it right after moving it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carrying a hygrometer from a warm room to a cool basement changes both the air and the sensor temperature. Readings can take 20 to 30 minutes to settle. Setting it down and reading it immediately gives a number that is often several percent wrong, usually in the reassuring direction.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting it on a cold exterior wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The inside face of an exterior wall is cooler than the room, so humidity measured right against it reads higher. A hygrometer there makes a room seem damper than it is and can lead to overdrying. Mount it on an interior wall or shelf, away from sun, vents and appliances.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an analog dial for real decisions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dial hygrometers are attractive and often wildly inaccurate, commonly 5 to 10 percent off and hard to calibrate. They are fine as decoration but not for deciding whether a basement needs a dehumidifier or a crawl space is growing mold. A budget digital unit is far more reliable for less money.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most accurate type of home hygrometer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A digital hygrometer with a capacitive sensor rated at plus or minus 2 percent is the most accurate type most homeowners will use. Analog dial models are far less reliable. Accuracy also depends on calibration, so salt test any hygrometer when it arrives and once a year, and apply any offset you find to its readings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a basement be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for below 50 percent in the open basement and never let it sit above 60 percent for long. Because the foundation walls are cooler than the room, humidity right against them runs higher than the middle of the space, so a 45 to 50 percent target leaves a safety margin against condensation and mold on cold surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my hygrometer is accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the salt test. Mix table salt with a little water into a damp paste, seal it in a bag or container with the hygrometer and leave it overnight at a steady temperature. The air should settle at 75 percent. The difference between 75 and what your hygrometer shows is its error, which you can add or subtract going forward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a smart hygrometer worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For basements, crawl spaces and any space you do not check every day, yes. Logging reveals overnight spikes that a glance at a screen misses, and Wi-Fi alerts catch a failed dehumidifier or a wet crawl space within minutes. For a bedroom or living room you are in daily, a simple standalone model is usually enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hygrometers do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Humidity varies by floor and room, so one per level is a sensible minimum: one in the basement, one on the main floor and one in a bedroom. Add one in the crawl space if you have one. Budget digital units are inexpensive enough that measuring several spaces costs less than one mid-range smart sensor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a hygrometer detect mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A hygrometer measures the moisture in the air, not mold itself. It is still one of the best mold prevention tools, because mold needs sustained humidity above about 60 percent on a surface to grow. Keeping your readings in the 30 to 50 percent range removes the conditions mold depends on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace a hygrometer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no fixed schedule. Check it with a salt test each year, and if the error keeps growing or becomes erratic between tests, replace it. Budget digital sensors often stay usable for several years in normal rooms. Units kept in very damp basements or crawl spaces tend to drift faster and may need replacing sooner.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

